data.content:
Since there's no `.csproj` visible, here's the lightest option depending on your target framework:

---

**If targeting .NET 8+** — use the built-in `TimeProvider` class (zero new abstractions needed):

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? time = null)
        => _time = time ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider`:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);
Assert.IsTrue(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

(`Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package provides `FakeTimeProvider`.)

---

**If targeting < .NET 8** — a one-line `Func` parameter is even lighter:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly Func<DateTimeOffset> _utcNow;

    public InvoiceCalculator(Func<DateTimeOffset>? utcNow = null)
        => _utcNow = utcNow ?? (() => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow);

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _utcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

---

Both approaches keep the default parameter so existing callers don't break and no DI container is needed. Would you like me to apply one of these to your file?
